WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.327
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:09.037
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:18.061
Unit fifty-nine: Leadership in Crisis. In this unit, you will analyse what makes leaders effective in a crisis.

4
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.748
crisis communication.

5
00:00:21.448 --> 00:00:27.801
crisis communication means how organisations share information during an emergency.

6
00:00:28.101 --> 00:00:30.786
Clear crisis communication prevents panic.

7
00:00:31.486 --> 00:00:34.405
Your turn. Say: crisis communication.

8
00:00:37.105 --> 00:00:38.745
crisis communication.

9
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.204
decisive action.

10
00:00:41.904 --> 00:00:45.297
decisive action means quick and firm decisions.

11
00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:48.490
Decisive action saved the company from collapse.

12
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.329
Your turn. Say: decisive action.

13
00:00:56.029 --> 00:00:57.366
decisive action.

14
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.222
situational awareness.

15
00:01:00.922 --> 00:01:05.301
situational awareness means understanding what is happening around you.

16
00:01:05.601 --> 00:01:08.634
Pilots need excellent situational awareness.

17
00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:13.240
Your turn. Say: situational awareness.

18
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.743
situational awareness.

19
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.169
stakeholder trust.

20
00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:25.562
stakeholder trust means confidence from everyone affected by an organisation.

21
00:01:25.862 --> 00:01:28.036
Honesty builds stakeholder trust.

22
00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:32.096
Your turn. Say: stakeholder trust.

23
00:01:34.796 --> 00:01:36.343
stakeholder trust.

24
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.711
damage control.

25
00:01:39.411 --> 00:01:43.814
damage control means actions to limit harm after something goes wrong.

26
00:01:44.114 --> 00:01:47.193
The company went into damage control after the leak.

27
00:01:47.893 --> 00:01:51.149
Your turn. Say: damage control.

28
00:01:53.849 --> 00:01:55.187
damage control.

29
00:01:56.183 --> 00:01:57.777
contingency leadership.

30
00:01:58.477 --> 00:02:03.402
contingency leadership means leading flexibly according to changing circumstances.

31
00:02:03.702 --> 00:02:06.944
Contingency leadership adapts to each situation.

32
00:02:07.644 --> 00:02:11.225
Your turn. Say: contingency leadership.

33
00:02:13.925 --> 00:02:15.518
contingency leadership.

34
00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:17.750
clear messaging.

35
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.773
clear messaging means simple, consistent communication.

36
00:02:22.073 --> 00:02:25.315
Clear messaging helped people follow safety rules.

37
00:02:26.015 --> 00:02:29.352
Your turn. Say: clear messaging.

38
00:02:32.052 --> 00:02:33.216
clear messaging.

39
00:02:34.217 --> 00:02:35.554
command structure.

40
00:02:36.254 --> 00:02:40.100
command structure means the system showing who makes which decisions.

41
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.154
A clear command structure avoids confusion.

42
00:02:43.854 --> 00:02:47.261
Your turn. Say: command structure.

43
00:02:49.961 --> 00:02:51.252
command structure.

44
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.668
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.968 --> 00:02:59.103
Rithy, what separates good leaders from bad ones in a crisis?

46
00:03:00.417 --> 00:03:04.683
Good leaders don't bury their heads in the sand. They take decisive action.

47
00:03:05.983 --> 00:03:08.180
But acting too fast can backfire.

48
00:03:09.483 --> 00:03:13.740
True. They need situational awareness first. It's a fine line.

49
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.365
And communication?

50
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:24.644
Clear messaging is crucial. If leaders say one thing and do another, stakeholder trust goes out of the window.

51
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.683
What about admitting mistakes?

52
00:03:28.983 --> 00:03:33.723
Owning up early is better damage control than trying to sweep problems under the carpet.

53
00:03:35.017 --> 00:03:38.584
Grammar focus: Idiomatic language (mastery).

54
00:03:38.884 --> 00:03:41.324
Idioms make analysis vivid in speech.

55
00:03:41.624 --> 00:03:46.503
Good leaders don't bury their heads in the sand, and they don't sweep problems under the carpet.

56
00:03:46.803 --> 00:03:49.929
Without honesty, trust goes out of the window.

57
00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:57.047
Idioms make spoken analysis vivid; in formal reports, use 'ignore', 'disappear' and 'conceal' instead.

58
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.701
Now read and listen. Leading When Everything Goes Wrong.

59
00:04:02.001 --> 00:04:05.521
Crises test leaders in ways that ordinary times never do.

60
00:04:06.817 --> 00:04:11.092
Research on crisis leadership highlights a few consistent qualities.

61
00:04:12.383 --> 00:04:16.589
Effective leaders combine situational awareness with decisive action.

62
00:04:17.883 --> 00:04:22.333
They establish a clear command structure so that everyone knows their role.

63
00:04:23.617 --> 00:04:28.925
Clear messaging, repeated consistently, reduces fear and confusion.

64
00:04:30.217 --> 00:04:34.689
Leaders who sweep problems under the carpet usually lose stakeholder trust.

65
00:04:35.983 --> 00:04:40.444
By contrast, those who admit mistakes early often recover more quickly.

66
00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:47.616
As one expert put it, a crisis does not build character; it reveals it.

67
00:04:48.917 --> 00:04:51.465
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:51.765 --> 00:04:55.471
Actions to limit harm after something goes wrong are blank.

69
00:04:55.771 --> 00:05:01.050
A, damage control. B, command structure. C, clear messaging.

70
00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:08.396
The answer is A: damage control.

71
00:05:09.883 --> 00:05:12.893
To ignore a problem is to bury your head in the blank.

72
00:05:13.193 --> 00:05:16.440
A, ground. B, sand. C, water.

73
00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.123
The answer is B: sand.

74
00:05:24.617 --> 00:05:27.475
To hide a problem is to sweep it under the blank.

75
00:05:27.775 --> 00:05:31.313
A, carpet. B, table. C, bed.

76
00:05:35.317 --> 00:05:37.839
The answer is A: carpet.

77
00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.116
Understanding what is happening around you is blank.

78
00:05:42.416 --> 00:05:48.554
A, situational awareness. B, stakeholder trust. C, decisive action.

79
00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:56.001
The answer is A: situational awareness.

80
00:05:57.517 --> 00:06:01.304
According to the expert, what does a crisis do to character?

81
00:06:01.604 --> 00:06:06.140
A, builds it. B, reveals it. C, destroys it.

82
00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:13.636
The answer is B: reveals it.

83
00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:20.333
Now it's your turn. Part one: Describe a leader who handled a crisis well or badly.

84
00:06:25.633 --> 00:06:30.849
Part two: Use two idioms, describe what they did, and evaluate the result.

85
00:06:37.149 --> 00:06:49.475
During a flood in my province, the district chief didn't bury his head in the sand. He gave clear messages every day and admitted when supplies were late. Because he never swept problems under the carpet, people trusted him.

86
00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:52.622
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:53.617 --> 00:06:58.533
Great work today! You learned eight new words and idiomatic language (mastery).

88
00:06:58.833 --> 00:07:02.820
Today's value is courage. Try new things and be brave.

89
00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.808
See you in Unit sixty. Goodbye!

90
00:07:06.108 --> 00:07:09.526
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

